FINAL OWNER DIRECTIVE — FIX THE LAST BLOCKER: LLM PATCH GENERATION HANG

The QA system restoration is accepted as good progress.

However, IVX IA is still NOT complete as an autonomous senior developer because the LLM patch-generation stage hangs and cannot complete even a simple create_file task.

Do not create more QA reports.

Do not add more historical evidence.

Focus only on fixing the code-generation execution path.

==================================================
CONFIRMED FAILURE
==================================================

Current live failure:

- Worker job starts successfully.
- Status reaches RUNNING.
- Progress reaches approximately 10%.
- Stage remains:
  "Generating technical plan + patch via IVX LLM"
- Execution hangs for more than 214 seconds.
- No valid patch is returned.
- No file is created.
- No commit is produced.
- No deployment occurs.
- Acceptance Steps 7–11 fail.

This is the final critical blocker.

==================================================
1. ADD STAGE-LEVEL OBSERVABILITY
==================================================

Instrument the complete LLM patch-generation flow.

Record timestamps for:

- task received;
- repository context collection;
- target-file selection;
- prompt construction;
- LLM request started;
- first token received;
- response completed;
- JSON parsed;
- patch validated;
- patch applied;
- tests started;
- tests completed.

Expose:

- request ID;
- model;
- provider;
- timeout;
- input token estimate;
- output token count;
- retry number;
- response status;
- parser status;
- exact sanitized error;
- heartbeat.

Do not show only “generating patch.”

Show exactly where it is hanging.

==================================================
2. ADD HARD TIMEOUTS AND CANCELLATION
==================================================

Implement bounded timeouts:

- planning request: 45 seconds;
- patch-generation request: 90 seconds;
- repair request: 90 seconds;
- total generation stage: 180 seconds maximum.

When timeout occurs:

1. Cancel the provider request.
2. Persist failure evidence.
3. Increment retry count.
4. Reduce context size if necessary.
5. Retry with a smaller structured request.
6. After maximum retries, mark FAILED with exact reason.

Never leave a job RUNNING indefinitely.

==================================================
3. SPLIT PLANNING FROM PATCH GENERATION
==================================================

Do not ask one LLM call to:

- inspect the repository;
- reason about architecture;
- create a full plan;
- generate multiple files;
- output one large JSON payload.

Use separate stages:

STAGE A — TASK PLAN
Return:
- target files;
- files to inspect;
- changes required;
- tests required;
- risks.

STAGE B — FILE CONTEXT
Load only the selected files and relevant dependencies.

STAGE C — PATCH GENERATION
Generate one file patch at a time.

STAGE D — PATCH VALIDATION
Validate syntax and expected structure.

STAGE E — TEST REPAIR
Use compiler/test errors for targeted repair.

This must work incrementally.

==================================================
4. USE STRUCTURED OUTPUT
==================================================

Require strict structured output.

Example:

{
  "taskSummary": "...",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "backend/api/example.ts",
      "operation": "create",
      "content": "..."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "backend/api/example.test.ts",
      "operation": "create",
      "content": "..."
    }
  ],
  "commands": [
    "bun test backend/api/example.test.ts"
  ]
}

Validate:

- valid JSON;
- known operation;
- safe path;
- non-empty content;
- no binary data;
- no absolute paths;
- no secret exposure;
- size limits.

If parsing fails, retry with a repair prompt.

==================================================
5. REDUCE CONTEXT INTELLIGENTLY
==================================================

Do not send 30,000 characters blindly.

Build context from:

- target route;
- registration file;
- related service;
- imports;
- tests;
- types;
- nearby code;
- coding conventions.

Use repository search to identify relevant files.

For simple create_file tasks, send only:

- task;
- target directory;
- nearby example file;
- expected export;
- test convention.

Do not include unrelated files.

==================================================
6. REMOVE BAD FALLBACK INTERCEPTION
==================================================

Audit deterministicPilotFallback and every fallback path.

Rules:

- fallback may handle trivial deterministic edits only;
- fallback must not intercept non-trivial tasks;
- fallback must not return empty changes;
- fallback must not mark success without filesChanged;
- fallback must not bypass the real LLM path silently.

If fallback cannot complete the task, return control to the main generation pipeline.

==================================================
7. ADD PROVIDER FAILOVER
==================================================

When the primary LLM call:

- times out;
- returns empty content;
- returns invalid JSON;
- returns truncated output;
- returns provider 5xx;
- returns rate limit;

then retry using:

1. same provider with smaller context;
2. alternate model;
3. alternate gateway/provider if configured.

Persist which provider completed the patch.

No endless retry loop.

==================================================
8. SIMPLE ACCEPTANCE TEST FIRST
==================================================

Before testing full endpoint creation, run this minimal task from IVX IA Chat:

“Create backend/generated/ivx-proof-v1.ts exporting:
export const ivxProofVersion = 1;
Add a unit test confirming the value.”

Required result:

- new job ID;
- LLM request completes;
- file created;
- test created;
- typecheck passes;
- test passes;
- commit created;
- no deployment required for this first test;
- evidence returned.

If this fails, do not proceed to larger tasks.

==================================================
9. NON-TRIVIAL ACCEPTANCE TEST
==================================================

After the simple test passes, run:

“Create GET /api/ivx/developer-proof-v3 returning current SHA, worker version, deployment status and timestamp. Add tests, commit, deploy and verify live.”

Required proof:

- task ID;
- worker ID;
- selected files;
- prompt size;
- model/provider;
- generation duration;
- files created;
- files modified;
- tests passed;
- commit SHA;
- deployment ID;
- production SHA;
- HTTP 200 live response;
- evidence ID.

==================================================
10. FAILURE-RECOVERY TEST
==================================================

Force one LLM request to timeout.

Expected behavior:

- request cancelled;
- job not left RUNNING;
- retry starts;
- smaller context used;
- second attempt completes;
- no zombie job;
- no duplicate commit;
- task reaches COMPLETED or FAILED honestly.

==================================================
11. FINAL ACCEPTANCE
==================================================

Do not mark IVX IA Senior Developer VERIFIED until:

- simple create_file task passes;
- non-trivial endpoint task passes;
- patch generation completes within bounded time;
- no job remains stuck at 10%;
- retries work;
- failures are visible;
- filesChanged is non-empty;
- a new commit is produced;
- deployment and live verification succeed;
- evidence is returned in IVX IA Chat.

Final verdict must be one of:

A. PATCH GENERATION VERIFIED
B. SIMPLE CODE GENERATION VERIFIED
C. NON-TRIVIAL DEVELOPMENT VERIFIED
D. STILL INCOMPLETE

Only A + B + C together mean completion.

Execute this now.
